// 056 — Operator overloading — DESIGN NOTE, what not to do
//
// C++, Python, Ruby, and others let user code redefine what `+`, `*`,
// `==`, `<<`, etc. mean for custom types. Sounds elegant. In practice,
// a reader seeing `a + b` has no idea whether that's integer addition,
// vector concatenation, or (in C++ with `operator+=`) a side-effecting
// database write. The operator hides the operation.
//
// AiLang will never offer operator overloading. Every operation has
// exactly one name: Add for integers, StringConcat for strings, and
// the library exposes a distinct function per semantic. If you want
// to add vectors, the library call is `VecAdd(v1, v2)` — different
// name, different meaning, no surprise.
//
// The trade: slightly more typing; a much smaller mental model. Every
// call-site is self-documenting about the type of work being done.
LibraryImport.Arena

SubRoutine.Main {
    // Integer arithmetic: named explicitly
    n = Add(2, 3)
    PrintMessage("Add(2,3) = ")
    PrintNumber(n)
    PrintMessage("\n")

    // String concatenation: ALSO named explicitly — no overloaded +
    s = StringConcat("hello ", "world")
    PrintMessage(s)
    PrintMessage("\n")

    // If a hypothetical VecAdd existed, it would live under that name,
    // never overloading Add. Two different operations, two different
    // call-sites, zero ambiguity at read time.
}
RunTask(Main)
